{"chunk_id": "56de07e13616", "text": "This section recounts the famous debate of the young sage Ashtavakra in King Janaka\u2019s court. Despite being mocked for his age, Ashtavakra argues that wisdom\u2014not age or appearance\u2014defines true maturity. After gaining entry, he boldly challenges the renowned debater Vandin, whose defeats had led to Brahmanas being cast into water. Their contest unfolds as a rapid exchange of sacred enumerations\u2014cosmic, ritual, and philosophical correspondences from one to thirteen. When Vandin falters, Ashtavakra completes the verse and wins. Vandin reveals he is Varuna\u2019s son and had sent the defeated Brahmanas to Varuna\u2019s sacrifice; they return, including Ashtavakra\u2019s father Kahoda. Reunited, father and son celebrate the triumph. Bathing in the river Samanga miraculously straightens Ashtavakra\u2019s crooked limbs, symbolizing the restoration of truth and the vindication of wisdom over outward form.\nYoung sage Ashtavakra defeats the debater Vandin in Janaka\u2019s court through a numbered riddle contest, restores drowned Brahmanas, and reunites with his father.\nThemes: wisdom vs age, philosophical debate, Vedic symbolism of numbers, filial redemption, justice in royal court", "vector": [0.021220160648226738, -0.010080608539283276, 0.012961763888597488, 0.006161135621368885, -0.010788863524794579, 0.023750627413392067, -0.022567909210920334, -0.015169045887887478, -0.032951075583696365, -0.014660201966762543, 0.016901865601539612, 0.00785957369953394, -0.002090385416522622, 0.010720101185142994, -0.015320324338972569, -0.007557017728686333, 0.04329298064112663, -0.011256449855864048, 0.02355809323489666, -0.013559999875724316, -0.008450932800769806, 0.010912636294960976, -0.012624827213585377, 0.00823089201003313, -0.021467706188559532, 0.020573792979121208, 0.011820304207503796, -0.019033508375287056, 0.018387138843536377, -0.03088819421827793, 0.003237002994865179, -0.019253548234701157, -0.001944264629855752